<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duke Huan of Qi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 643 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duke Huan of Qi ruled the state of Qi in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 685 to 643 BCE. With his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a> he reformed government and the economy and became the first of the hegemons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a>. He led alliances of states against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong</a> and Di peoples and protected weaker states such as Xing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-qi.jpg" alt="Duke Huan of Qi" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qihuangongguanzhong2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Lü Xiaobai, a son of Duke Xi of Qi, he was not in line for the throne, since he had at least two older brothers, Zhu'er and Jiu. His tutor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-shuya">Bao Shuya</a>, fearing for his life under the troubled rule of Zhu'er as Duke Xiang, took him into exile in the state of Ju. After Duke Xiang was assassinated in 686 BCE and his successor Wuzhi was murdered a month later, Xiaobai hurried back and seized the government and army before Prince Jiu, backed by his tutor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a> and Duke Zhuang of Lu, could be installed. Lu's invading army was crushed at Qianshi, and Duke Zhuang executed Jiu and handed Guan Zhong over as a prisoner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-shuya">Bao Shuya</a>'s advice the duke spared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a> and made him chief minister. Together they divided the land and people into regulated units and enforced a merit-based system of government, which let Qi mobilise its resources more effectively than the other, loosely structured Zhou states. In 667 BCE the rulers of Lu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, Chen and Zheng chose him as their leader, and King Hui of Zhou named him hegemon, with authority to act militarily in the royal name.</p><p>He saved Yan, Xing and Wey from invasions by non-Zhou peoples and in 656 BCE led an alliance of eight states that defeated Cai, a satellite of Chu, then invaded Chu itself, which agreed to halt its northward expansion and attend a meeting at Shaoling, the first of its kind. The historian Cho-yun Hsu wrote that he set up an interstate order guarded by consensus rather than authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His power declined after Chu turned east, his alliance was beaten at Loulin in 645 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-zhong">Guan Zhong</a> died the same year. Six sons by different concubines fought over the succession. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzi">Guanzi</a> says four officials locked the ailing duke in his room and starved him, a story the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo Zhuan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> do not tell. After his death war broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linzi">Linzi</a>, his body lay unburied for between seven days and three months, and Qi lost its leading place to Jin under Duke Wen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duke Huan of Qi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Huang joined Qi's alliance under <b>Duke Huan</b> and refused to send tribute to the State of Chu, trusting in Qi's distant support.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-huang">State of Huang</a></li><li><blockquote>Jiang appears in the Zuo zhuan when, with the State of Huang, it joined the covenant led by <b>Duke Huan of Qi</b> in the 650s BCE, a gesture of defiance towards the State of Chu, its powerful southern neighbour.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-jiang">State of Jiang</a></li><li><blockquote>It gave refuge to <b>Prince Xiaobai of Qi</b>, who returned in 685 BCE to become Duke Huan, the first hegemon.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-ju">State of Ju</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Duke Huan of Qi</b> led a coalition to rescue it and resettled its people at Yiyi in 659 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-xing">State of Xing</a></li></ul></section>
